# ---- merge-time batch trains (BH-498, BH-1269) -------------------------------
# bin/batch-resolve.sh takes approved sibling PRs whose only conflicts are in
# files the repo itself marks machine-written, merges them into ONE union
# branch, regenerates once, runs the repo's verification contract once, and
# lands the lot with a merge commit so every member becomes merged by ancestry.
# One ticket, one PR, one review, always: batching is a merge-time optimization
# and never a review-time one.
#
# The lane, per project. 0 still settles a train already in flight; it only
# stops new ones. The fleet-wide switch is flags/lane-off-batch-resolve and the
# per-project one is flags/lane-off-batch-resolve-<project>.
#BATCH_RESOLVE=1
# MEMBERS A TRAIN NEEDS. One, by default (BH-1269): a train of one costs exactly
# the CI run a direct merge costs and keeps everything a train is for — the
# merge-tree probe, the one regenerate, and the closure that marks every member
# merged by ancestry. Before this, a lone approved PR with a registry conflict
# waited for a partner that often never came. Set 2 for the old behaviour.
#BATCH_TRAIN_MIN=1
# Members one train may carry, and how many levels of binary split a red union
# may buy before its members go back to the one-at-a-time path. Each split level
# costs one CI run per half, which is why the red-member reads below are worth
# more than raising it.
#BATCH_MAX_MEMBERS=8
#BATCH_SPLIT_MAX=2
# READ EACH CANDIDATE'S OWN CI BEFORE BUILDING THE UNION. A member whose head is
# already red sinks the whole train: on 2026-09-21 one PR's replay-evals job,
# red on a dead credential, took ten PRs down and bought a split to find out
# which. One check-runs read per candidate is cheaper than one CI run per half.
#BATCH_OWN_CI_RED=1
# AND AN INHERITED RED IS REBASED, NEVER RERUN. When main is red every open PR's
# own CI is red too — it ran against that main — and `rerun-failed-jobs` re-tests
# the SAME merge commit GitHub pinned the run to, so it comes back red for ever.
# The recovery is a PURE rebase onto the base: a fresh merge ref, a fresh run,
# and the head-bound verdict carried only when the patch's own added and removed
# lines are identical. Once per head, this many per PR, then the PR is parked.
# A rebase that comes out clean but whose union-merged RENDER no longer builds
# is treated as a real conflict: nothing is pushed, because a pushed head that
# is red for a new reason is no longer behind the base and would never be
# retried. The conductor's own rebase path, which regenerates, owns those.
#BATCH_REBASE_MAX=2
# CLOSE A TRAIN WHOSE MEMBER WENT RED AFTER IT FORMED, and re-form without that
# member on the same pass — instead of paying the red run and then the binary
# split that would rediscover what is already known.
#BATCH_PREEMPT_LATE_RED=1
# A MERGED fix-main-red PR LIFTS THE FREEZE HERE. Its own CI ran on the merge
# ref, which IS main's next state, so the hold is over the moment it merges
# rather than at bin/main-red-detector.sh's next pass over main — a wait that
# cost ~20 minutes per red episode, three times in one day. flags/main-red-<repo>
# and the D1 pause row go together, in one call. "Green" means a CONCLUDED
# SUCCESS run, never merely "nothing has failed yet": a head with no check runs,
# with any still queued or in flight, or with only skipped ones lifts nothing.
# The label is the fleet's (MC_MAIN_RED_LABEL), not a literal.
#BATCH_MAIN_RED_CLEAR=1
# The workflow run a head is judged green on, by NAME. Check-run counts lie:
# train #1229 merged on "0 pending, 6 ok" while its shard jobs were still
# waiting for a pool runner and had raised no check runs at all. A repo that
# raises no run of this name is judged on its check runs alone.
#BATCH_CI_WORKFLOW=Harness CI
# ...and must a run of it EXIST before a head counts as green? 1, the default,
# refuses a sha with none. That is this fleet's own shape: a push made with the
# App token raises no pull_request run, so a fix PR landed that way carries a
# Summary success, a neutral Mergify run and ZERO workflow runs — a head whose
# shards never ran, which the check-runs half alone would read as green and lift
# a fleet-wide freeze onto. Set 0 only for a repo that genuinely raises no
# workflow of this name, where requiring one makes its freezes unliftable.
#BATCH_CI_RUN_REQUIRED=1
# Check-run NAMES (an ERE) that are not this repo's CI and must never make a
# head look red OR pending: Mergify's own reporter, which stays pending until
# the queue takes the PR, and a mirror publish that fails on a leak guard.
# `^Mergify` and not a full name on purpose — that reporter has been called both
# "Mergify Merge Protections" and "Mergify Merge Queue", and a name that drifts
# out of this list makes a queued Mergify run read as "still pending", so the
# freeze lift never fires.
#BATCH_CI_IGNORE=^(Mergify|Publish share mirror)
# Lines kept in each (PR, head) exclusion file under state/batch-resolve/.
#BATCH_EXCLUDE_KEEP=2000
# TRAINS IN FLIGHT AT ONCE, ON DISJOINT PLANNED-FILE SETS (BH-1270). One train
# bounds the base branch's merge rate at one CI run, and nothing about that wait
# is a safety property: two unions cut from the same base only meet if they
# touch the same files. So the candidates are partitioned — any two that could
# ever conflict (intersecting planned-file sets, the same module directory, or
# either one with no list anybody can read) ride the SAME train — and one train
# per group is opened in one tick. When a group's train lands under another's,
# that one is RE-FORMED on the new tip: same PR, same members at the same heads,
# a fresh CI run and no re-review, because nothing a verdict is bound to moved.
# 1 is the pre-BH-1270 behaviour, byte for byte.
#TRAIN_PARALLEL=2
# ...BOUNDED BY THE CI POOL. N trains in flight raise N concurrent runs of the
# repo's workflow, and this repo's run is 8 shard jobs wide (two 4-way
# matrices). Two of those on a pool of 8 runners does not make anything faster:
# the second run's shards QUEUE, and a queued job is indistinguishable from a
# slow one (docs/CI-RUNNERS.md). 0 runners means nobody has declared a pool
# size, which is the honest default here — CI runs on the CI_RUNNER repo
# variable, hosted, whose concurrency is the account's and not ours to count —
# and then only TRAIN_PARALLEL binds. The effective cap is
# min(TRAIN_PARALLEL, floor(BATCH_CI_RUNNERS / BATCH_CI_SHARDS)).
#BATCH_CI_RUNNERS=0
#BATCH_CI_SHARDS=8
# RE-FORMS ONE TRAIN MAY BUY before its age budget owns it. Bounded because a
# repo whose base moves faster than a union verifies would otherwise re-form for
# ever and never reach its own queue. 0 turns re-forming off.
#BATCH_REFORM_MAX=3
# MODULE BOUNDARIES TWO TRAINS MAY NEVER BE SPLIT ACROSS. Two PRs editing
# different files of one module are a textual non-conflict and often a semantic
# one, and it is that module's own suite that would catch it — so they ride the
# same train rather than two at once. An entry ending in `*/` means the CHILD
# directory names the module (bin/lib/mc/, bin/lib/jobs/, bin/lib/rb/ are
# three); an entry ending in `/` means the directory itself is one module. A
# WIDER list is the safe direction: it merges groups, it never splits them.
#BATCH_MODULE_DIRS=bin/lib/*/ worker/jobs/src/routes/ console-ui/src/screens/
#
# NOT A KNOB, A FILE: state/batch-resolve/<project>/train-skip.txt holds one PR
# number per line and is read LIVE on every candidate, so an operator can park a
# member out of the next train by appending a line — nothing restarts, no flag
# is flipped, and deleting the line puts it back.
